// Package goasm is the goasm layer adapter: the "# TEMPLATE" blocks of
// *.goasm files, and the edge from each to the Go emitter method generated
// from it.
//
// goasm is a code generator, not an assembler: a template of assembly
// compiles to a method (*Emitter).Emit<Name> in a file headed "Code
// generated by goasm". That is the ADR 004 pattern — the same bridge as
// .proto → .pb.go and .sql → sqlc — and the Go layer already sees the
// generated side. This adapter supplies the source side and the link.
//
// The adapter owns no parser (ADR 000): every file goes through goasm's
// own template package, and the naming rule that joins a template to its
// method is goasm's gen.MethodName. What comes out:
//
//   - goasm_file nodes, keyed by repo-relative path (ADR 002 rule 7),
//     carrying the file's "# ALIAS" bindings; template nodes keyed by the
//     file and the template's name (rule 10), carrying the parameter
//     list, the return list and the instruction count;
//   - contains (EXTRACTED): file → template;
//   - generates (DECLARED, via=goasm): template → the Go method the
//     generator emits for it, in the package a "//go:generate" directive
//     names. The directive is the declaration: its -in names the template
//     file, its -out the generated file, and the file's package is the
//     module path plus the directory (as the SQL layer reads sqlc.yaml).
//     The edge drops at merge when the Go layer has no such method, as
//     every DECLARED edge does (ADR 006 rule 6).
//
// A template file no directive names gets its nodes and no generates
// edges: the target package would be a guess, and a DECLARED edge is
// exact or absent. The count of such templates is reported.
package goasm
